    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with a potent offensive profile, anchored by **Yordan Alvarez** (87.42) and **Trea Turner** (74.94). **Yordan Alvarez** is our clear standout, benefiting from a prime matchup against Montero and a stellar 0.448 xwOBA, while **Trea Turner** remains the engine of our stolen base strategy. Despite the opposing pitcher\u2019s handedness, **Trea Turner** is locked in at the leadoff spot to exploit Thornton\u2019s high xERA of 5.20. Alongside them, **Christian Yelich** (74.32) and **Kyle Schwarber** (74.54) provide critical efficiency in their respective matchups, with **Kyle Schwarber** also poised to exploit the favorable park and pitching conditions afforded by the Thornton pairing. **Casey Schmitt** (63.20) and **Colt Keith** (68.71) also see significant bumps in their daily scores, driven by aggressive baserunning opportunities against slow deliveries from Arrighetti, which keeps their value high despite variable park factors.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench today involved **Chase Meidroth** (44.70), who sat on the bubble but ultimately lacked the categorical ceiling provided by the starters. While **Chase Meidroth** offered a respectable floor, he lacked the specific platoon or power upside required to displace the high-scoring **Colt Keith** or **Casey Schmitt**. We are monitoring the status of **Caleb Durbin** (55.95) closely; his score reflects a solid matchup against Warren, but he carries a 35% rain risk that could compromise the efficiency of his at-bats. Meanwhile, **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (60.95) remains a calculated risk; while he faces the elite arm of Misiorowski (2.74 xERA), his elite sprint speed and leadoff placement make him indispensable for our pursuit of the SB category. We continue to hold **Hyeseong Kim** in the minors, awaiting an active roster return to bolster our middle infield depth.",
